          "name": "The three questions the brief put to the audit",
          "width": "fill_container",
          "stroke": "$--color-divider",
          "strokeWidth": {
            "top": 1
          },
          "strokeAlignment": "inner",
          "layout": "vertical",
          "gap": "$--spacing-lg",
          "padding": [
            "$--spacing-xl",
            0,
            0,
            0
          ],
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "frame",
              "id": "Rr6b4",
              "name": "Q / Do the four right-panel tabs share one h",
              "width": "fill_container",
              "layout": "vertical",
              "gap": "$--spacing-xs",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "id": "FU1C4",
                  "name": "Question",
                  "fill": "$--color-primary",
                  "textGrowth": "fixed-width",
                  "width": "fill_container",
                  "content": "Do the four right-panel tabs share one header shape, one row height, one empty state?",
                  "fontFamily": "$--font-ui",
                  "fontSize": "$--text-title",
                  "fontWeight": "600"
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "id": "zoE15",
                  "name": "Answer",
                  "fill": "$--color-secondary",
                  "textGrowth": "fixed-width",
                  "width": "fill_container",
                  "content": "No, on all three. One header shape by accident only: all four get PanelHeader, at 44pt and inset 14, which nothing else in the shell uses. Row heights are 22 / 22 / 70 / ragged. Empty states are HideEmptyState three times and a left-aligned caption once. The right one is Changes: 22pt rows at inset 12, a group header with a count and a disclosure, a real HideEmptyState, and a selection that is a token surface at a token radius. It is right because it reaches for no literal of its own, and because its two-group shape proves the section header has to carry a count and a detail.",
                  "fontFamily": "$--font-ui",
                  "fontSize": "$--text-subhead",
                  "fontWeight": "normal"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "frame",
              "id": "H2LcBX",
              "name": "Q / Do Projects and Agents agree on row anat",
              "width": "fill_container",
              "layout": "vertical",
              "gap": "$--spacing-xs",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "id": "Fqi3B",
                  "name": "Question",
                  "fill": "$--color-primary",
                  "textGrowth": "fixed-width",
                  "width": "fill_container",
                  "content": "Do Projects and Agents agree on row anatomy?",
                  "fontFamily": "$--font-ui",
                  "fontSize": "$--text-title",
                  "fontWeight": "600"
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "id": "YNHdX",
                  "name": "Answer",
                  "fill": "$--color-secondary",
                  "textGrowth": "fixed-width",
                  "width": "fill_container",
                  "content": "They share AgentRow, so the anatomy agrees. What disagrees is what the row means: in Agents, line 1 is the project and line 2 the task; nested under a project tree, line 1 is the task and the project is gone. The same object shows a different identity depending on its container, so the operator cannot scan one lane down the column. Verdict: one density; the project name becomes a qualifier on line 3 beside the branch, present only when the container does not already imply it.",
                  "fontFamily": "$--font-ui",
                  "fontSize": "$--text-subhead",
                  "fontWeight": "normal"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "frame",
              "id": "b11l9",
              "name": "Q / Where does a panel state something the o",
              "width": "fill_container",
              "layout": "vertical",
              "gap": "$--spacing-xs",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "id": "uQ8sm",
                  "name": "Question",
                  "fill": "$--color-primary",
                  "textGrowth": "fixed-width",
                  "width": "fill_container",
                  "content": "Where does a panel state something the operator could have read from the row itself?",
                  "fontFamily": "$--font-ui",
                  "fontSize": "$--text-title",
                  "fontWeight": "600"
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "id": "mHwWh",
                  "name": "Answer",
                  "fill": "$--color-secondary",
                  "textGrowth": "fixed-width",
                  "width": "fill_container",
                  "content": "Finding 15: the pane count and the second timestamp in Git, and the whole Overview inspector.",
                  "fontFamily": "$--font-ui",
                  "fontSize": "$--text-subhead",
                  "fontWeight": "normal"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
